00:15:49.220 --> 00:15:58.690
Csaba: Yeah, so RowDAS, which is in the Heritage list, it's about distributed… globally construction.

112
00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:01.079
Csaba: Which we don't have now.

113
00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:04.590
Csaba: So, a quick history, behind it, let me just…

114
00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:07.700
Csaba: Change my screen so that I see my own slides.

115
00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:26.439
Csaba: Yeah, so, quick history behind this. So, the 4 DAS, which has… was 2 years ago, and then it had a version 2. It had a few things, but, what's interesting here is… is three features, which it introduced compared to… to PeerDAS. One was,

116
00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:38.419
Csaba: that we can do a distributed reconstruction in the network, and then there was, the block level, so the vertical coding, and then the subdinal sampling.

117
00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:47.270
Csaba: And RowDAS is basically there to bring the first one to the mainnet. So, distributed reconstruction.

118
00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:54.930
Csaba: PeerDAS also had a bunch of other things, bitmap-based messaging,

119
00:16:55.160 --> 00:17:07.270
Csaba: structured segment IDs, et cetera, et cetera, and we have now the PSO messages in gossip sub, so what I was building for Ludas is kind of using that and building on top of that.

120
00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:10.889
Csaba: So when I say distributed reconstruction.

121
00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:15.200
Csaba: That kind of means two things.

122
00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:18.989
Csaba: So one is the vertical distribution?

123
00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:32.410
Csaba: And I typically introduced it in that direction. So that means what you have now is every supernet node has the duty to do the reconstruction, so it has the columns, and then it has to calculate

124
00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:38.469
Csaba: The thing that it has to calculate it for every single blob, that it doesn't have some card blobs.

125
00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:57.720
Csaba: So they just repeat this work. And the aim of RowDAS in this regard, is to… to change that, change that by assigning duties, basically, to, to, to Sprints, and then they know which one is the duty, and they… they first act on that.

126
00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:06.150
Csaba: So by this, they are distributing loading network. And the other one is the horizontal, distributed reconstruction.

127
00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:21.330
Csaba: Which is more about, what ethereum should be. So, that's the, the supernodeless reconstruction. So you don't need, super nodes, but we have them, but you don't need them, and the protocol can still reconstruct,

128
00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:31.850
Csaba: blobs… blob content in the… in the distribution, so making the distribution more robust, even if you don't have these data centers, super nodes which are supporting it.

129
00:18:34.040 --> 00:18:37.900
Csaba: Yeah, a bit about numbers.

130
00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:45.180
Csaba: So… currently in the… in mainnet, we have more than 2,000 super nodes. It's kind of…

131
00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:47.629
Csaba: They're coming from different…

132
00:18:48.410 --> 00:18:58.199
Csaba: parts. Part of them is, is, is high custody, high custody. They were high custody. Part of them is high-stake notes, another one is… is Layer 2 notes, which needs the blobs.

133
00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:10.490
Csaba: And then each one of them has to do with the construction. The construction is expensive because of the construction of the k commitments. It's like 160 milliseconds, ballpark number.

134
00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:22.219
Csaba: And then you do it on every blob, let's say on 32 blobs, then you are spending a bunch of CPU time and everyone is spending it, and by distributing it among the nodes, you can just reduce that spend at the network level.

135
00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:38.010
Csaba: There is already something in PeerDAS, I was adding it to the specification, so it's… it has this kind of allowed random delay, and that can help mitigate this, but it's not to the level of kind of dedicated reconstruction.

136
00:19:39.650 --> 00:19:58.339
Csaba: So what you have in RowDAS is basically everyone has a dedicated law. It's kind of the custody law, but it's not custody requirement. I didn't want to make it custody requirement, because that would change too many things, and that would be too big of a change, I guess. So, no custody law, just an add-on law.

137
00:19:58.570 --> 00:20:00.490
Csaba: Which you should care about.

138
00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:04.549
Csaba: And that kind of designates which… what you do first.

139
00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:09.619
Csaba: And then you have the low topic, on which you can actually send out that data.

140
00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:18.069
Csaba: So you are super node, ID something, based on that, you know that you are responsible for, low…

141
00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:31.050
Csaba: 58, and those 58 maps to blob number 3, and then there's a blob number 3, then you are reconstructing it. There's this mapping click, between blobs and… and those, where I'm keeping

142
00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:45.909
Csaba: 128 lows, we can discuss that constant, it can be different. But the number of lows, and there is a mapping between, blocks in the block and loads, so that we are evening out load in the network, on the…

143
00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:47.540
Csaba: On the longer term.

144
00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:55.600
Csaba: So this is how… how the construction works. Basically, you have your, you're, you assigned.

145
00:20:56.600 --> 00:21:14.790
Csaba: And then, that's kind of your duty, and you should, if you see that you have to deconstruct, you are deconstructing that, you are sharing, the result of that along the row, and then, if you still see that there's nothing else deconstructed, then you do reconstruct the other things.

146
00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:18.079
Csaba: And then, actually, other nodes can chip in even later.

147
00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:22.360
Csaba: Yeah.

148
00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:37.169
Csaba: So the… on the deconstruction node, this means basically the two others of magnitude, improvement. So instead of deconstructing everything, you are just reconstructing 128, if you have a supernode, 128…

149
00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:47.110
Csaba: Of that, so you just spend much less CPU time doing this reconstruction work. And if the network has, enough nodes.

150
00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:53.470
Csaba: Which we have, then everything that's deconstructed by this, by this, deconstruction.

151
00:21:54.540 --> 00:22:05.350
Csaba: If the network doesn't have enough loads, or things are delayed, then the thing is falling back, basically, to what we have in PBS. So then you are falling back to deconstructing other things as well.

152
00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:15.260
Csaba: And then there is one more fallback step, which is falling back to nodes are reconstructing without relying on zipper notes.

153
00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:21.990
Csaba: Okay. I tried to go fast, but feel free to ask.

154
00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:35.789
Csaba: Yeah, so the whole thing is designed in a way that kind of the column pass distribution becomes the… stays the canonical distribution.

155
00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:47.339
Csaba: And the low pass is just chipping in when things are not working. So, I was trying to design it in a way that, that the column pass

156
00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:50.209
Csaba: Remains as performant as it was before.

157
00:22:51.280 --> 00:23:09.240
Csaba: It's ballpark there, so I was measuring a few milliseconds up and down, measuring it over a bunch of network shapes and, network sizes and topologies, and the way, which is how it is implemented now, it…

158
00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:16.940
Csaba: achieves this. So we are not giving up on… on the performance, what we have, to…

159
00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:24.290
Csaba: to make the lows work. In my first naive implementation, the lows were just basically doubling the,

160
00:23:24.440 --> 00:23:32.690
Csaba: the, the data mount, and that's obviously not good, so… so I had to tune the implementation a lot, to do that.

161
00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:51.990
Csaba: But I tuned it, and now I have that. Yeah, this is kind of… that's an interesting figure, but, testing that the low-wise deconstruction happens, actually, which PBS is not doing it, and RowDAS is doing it, so it is just testing if

162
00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:58.880
Csaba: if there are the only notes… if there are no super nodes in the system, I was testing the reconstruction is working, and it's working.

163
00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:07.980
Csaba: Depending on the number of nodes, the, the custody's distribution, etc, maybe not at the 64 limit, but, at 65, it does.

164
00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:14.860
Csaba: And then I was doing a bunch of measurements,

165
00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:18.870
Csaba: I was writing under the tech, obviously it is not

166
00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:36.579
Csaba: the kind of mapping up all the attacks, but trying to do a few things that can happen, evaluating under different scenarios, PeerDAS against RowDAS, without super nodes, with 10% of super nodes, with 20% of super nodes, in the network.

167
00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:44.070
Csaba: And then evaluating, different sized consumptions. So, evaluating, basically how much

168
00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:49.820
Csaba: CPU time is spent, and then, how much

169
00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:56.570
Csaba: What was kind of the completion time, so the latency, and then the bytes sent on the network.

170
00:24:56.910 --> 00:25:02.540
Csaba: With… with an… with… Piotas and Envy RowDAS, and the…

171
00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:15.140
