700
01:28:15.870 --> 01:28:31.579
Raúl Kripalani: So, I'm happy to kind of, like, after we've… I finalized this design, to kind of, like, give another… another update. But basically, the design targets for how we're going to do this is, first of all, we want to use a single quick transfer and endpoint for both, EIP2P and EIP2P.

701
01:28:31.740 --> 01:28:41.619
Raúl Kripalani: We want to make sure that every connection that arrives or that we make, through that transport can serve both ETH B2B2P and ETH B2P streams.

702
01:28:41.750 --> 01:28:49.619
Raúl Kripalani: We want to make sure that we remain fully backwards compatible, so that EIP2Bonly peers notice nothing, absolutely nothing.

703
01:28:49.630 --> 01:29:02.350
Raúl Kripalani: And we want to make sure that it's non-invasive for existing CL code, or, like, minimally and contained at specifically the networking layer and nothing else, right? So it shouldn't percolate back up into the application. So…

704
01:29:02.680 --> 01:29:18.740
Raúl Kripalani: just to kind of give you an overview of how, for example, Prysm enables quick today, so we all know there's a host in the B2B that is fed options, and if enable QUIC, which is a flag in… a configuration flag if Prysm is enabled, which by default is true.

705
01:29:18.740 --> 01:29:22.829
Raúl Kripalani: then it adds the transport. Now, if you notice, qua…

706
01:29:22.830 --> 01:29:27.289
Raúl Kripalani: Prysm is never constructing the quick transport, and that is what needs to change here.

707
01:29:27.290 --> 01:29:34.630
Raúl Kripalani: So, what we need to do is, the application needs to own the transport and then inject it into both

708
01:29:34.660 --> 01:29:50.359
Raúl Kripalani: LIPI2P and ETHP2P. And we can add sugar so that it's, like, very, very simple, but kind of like the… the mechanics would be something like this. So, you have… this is ETHP2P, by the way, so this, you call… is already implemented, you get

709
01:29:50.390 --> 01:29:57.799
Raúl Kripalani: the… you call newShared on the transport, with the node key, and so on, and the connection that you've already created, and then from the shared.

710
01:29:58.080 --> 01:30:01.510
Raúl Kripalani: transport, you take the ETH P2P view.

711
01:30:01.630 --> 01:30:04.640
Raúl Kripalani: And that you would pass through EP2P.

712
01:30:04.710 --> 01:30:23.080
Raúl Kripalani: And then you take the lip E2P view, which is, somewhere around here, because the code is a little bit verbose. Yeah, here. Should have probably put it in a variable at the top so it was easier to follow. You take that, and you use this lent transport. In the case of GoLIPI2P, the… there is…

713
01:30:23.130 --> 01:30:32.910
Raúl Kripalani: This option that already allows the application to inject a transport into the quick transport through this, like, weird, quick reuse thing.

714
01:30:32.910 --> 01:30:46.409
Raúl Kripalani: That exists, and it's a little bit unergonomic to use. If this bothers us, we can change it. But there's only, like, one tiny patch needed, which is already sitting in this branch. I haven't opened a PR for it, because it is…

715
01:30:46.420 --> 01:30:50.550
Raúl Kripalani: built on the version of GoPTP that Prysm uses today.

716
01:30:50.570 --> 01:31:01.380
Raúl Kripalani: which is no longer, like, what exists on master and go B2BP, so it's… I don't think anybody's gonna care about this, but I can submit a patch, and I don't… I can't remember if there's a release

717
01:31:01.460 --> 01:31:11.510
Raúl Kripalani: like, a release branch for this lineage, or this… this… this old release, so I wouldn't know what the target would be of that PR. So it's just currently sitting, sitting there.

718
01:31:11.720 --> 01:31:17.819
Raúl Kripalani: And then, from the transport weekend, like, ETP2P can already, like, accept and dial, so…

719
01:31:17.950 --> 01:31:23.770
Raúl Kripalani: Now, how… what does this do behind the scenes? It solves the problem of…

720
01:31:23.770 --> 01:31:39.329
Raúl Kripalani: We can't just take a single quick transport and just give that raw transport to both stacks, because they're gonna collide and they're gonna conflict when they're listening, because they will both try to listen for connections, and then for every connection, they will try to accept streams.

721
01:31:39.330 --> 01:31:48.129
Raúl Kripalani: So they're… they're gonna conflict, and they're gonna race with one another. So what we need to do is, we need to identify the capabilities of the remote host.

722
01:31:48.140 --> 01:31:54.450
Raúl Kripalani: And, route incoming streams to the right listener when we get incoming streams, right?

723
01:31:54.690 --> 01:32:13.639
Raúl Kripalani: So, just to kind of, like, set the stage for how this works, and this, like, dispatching, and routing works, it's actually very simple, and it's based on… on a few… on a few facts that all come together. So, first of all, every ETP2P peer will also be a B2P peer.

724
01:32:14.070 --> 01:32:28.859
Raúl Kripalani: It's an Ethereum client. Therefore, at the time that parts of EIP2P begin to be adopted, lipid will still be there, right? So, it's not like it's gonna be a swap, it's gonna be a progressive… it would be a progressive upgrade, right?

725
01:32:29.070 --> 01:32:34.290
Raúl Kripalani: So, both… so an ETH P2P peer will also be a P2P peer.

726
01:32:35.050 --> 01:32:42.640
Raúl Kripalani: a legacy B2Bonly peer will never advertise ETH B2B, so we know that it's legacy.

727
01:32:43.290 --> 01:32:51.049
Raúl Kripalani: Now, the LIPI2P handshake is pretty… the TLS handshake is pretty straightforward, and it's very easy to extract.

728
01:32:51.300 --> 01:32:55.499
Raúl Kripalani: It's a single file. I've extracted it into a single file, so it's very easy.

729
01:32:55.930 --> 01:32:57.910
Raúl Kripalani: And,

730
01:32:58.370 --> 01:33:07.780
Raúl Kripalani: the idea… well, this should not go here, this is part of the design, but the, the other fact is that Libby2P does not support unidirectional streams.

731
01:33:09.200 --> 01:33:16.859
Raúl Kripalani: And it doesn't have any API at the B2BP level to do that. All streams are bi-directional. And in quick.

732
01:33:17.140 --> 01:33:25.250
Raúl Kripalani: streams… the directionality of the stream is determined, or is signaled, by the ID of the stream.

733
01:33:25.430 --> 01:33:41.749
Raúl Kripalani: So you apply a bit mask, and you know what kind of stream it is, and the Golet B2BP does this for you. QuickGo does this for you, so you don't need to do any of this parsing. But the point is that we know that if a unidirectional stream arrives, we know it's for EIP, but we don't want to depend only on that.

734
01:33:42.130 --> 01:33:52.389
Raúl Kripalani: Because, even though EP2P today only uses a unidirectional stream to prevent the… to deal with the simultaneous open problem at the stream level,

735
01:33:53.250 --> 01:33:56.929
Raúl Kripalani: But we don't want to deal with that. Like, we don't want to rely only on that.

736
01:33:57.160 --> 01:34:03.980
Raúl Kripalani: The most robust way is to actually peek at the first byte of every stream.

737
01:34:04.180 --> 01:34:06.680
Raúl Kripalani: And they're gonna be structured exactly this way.

738
01:34:07.080 --> 01:34:22.370
Raúl Kripalani: So with… for libi2P, we're gonna have a UVARINT, which is a length-limited prefix pull-up protocol selector, and then after that, the next… the next byte is gonna be a slash, because that's how multi-stream select protocols, start.

739
01:34:22.820 --> 01:34:32.799
Raúl Kripalani: So then what we do is we reserve this character and say, well, the first effective payload, or the first effective byte of a stream, is a slash.

740
01:34:32.920 --> 01:34:38.640
Raúl Kripalani: then we know it's lip, and the slash is reserved, so it can never be used in a neat P2P.

741
01:34:39.730 --> 01:34:44.370
Raúl Kripalani: So that is at the… at the stream level, at the stream dispatching level.

742
01:34:45.240 --> 01:34:56.590
Raúl Kripalani: The other thing that we need to know is whether the other party supports… if we are an EF P2P peer, does the other party support, support EF P2P or not?

743
01:34:56.890 --> 01:35:03.640
Raúl Kripalani: So with that, we're using, basically in the TLS 1.3 handshake, we advertise a new ELPN,

744
01:35:03.750 --> 01:35:09.209
Raúl Kripalani: We put it first, so that it takes precedence in the negotiation.

745
01:35:09.450 --> 01:35:18.070
Raúl Kripalani: And, both quick endpoints will agree on the first shared ALPM.

746
01:35:18.260 --> 01:35:22.360
Raúl Kripalani: So, if they both support ETH P2P, then that would be…

747
01:35:22.680 --> 01:35:29.400
Raúl Kripalani: A peer link, basically, that supports both stacks.

748
01:35:29.520 --> 01:35:36.630
Raúl Kripalani: if the… if we settle on Libby2P, then we know that we're just… it's just Libby2P, so…

749
01:35:36.750 --> 01:35:45.480
Raúl Kripalani: In the case of… in this one, in this case, in theIPI2P case, we take the raw quick connection and just get it to lip E2P, that's the end of it.

750
01:35:46.230 --> 01:35:52.849
Raúl Kripalani: In the case that we agree on ETHB2P, then we split the connection into two views.

751
01:35:53.200 --> 01:35:58.629
Raúl Kripalani: So, we split it into the ETH P2P view, which will handle only ETH P2P streams.

752
01:35:58.750 --> 01:36:02.939
Raúl Kripalani: by the EF B2BP stack, And into the E2P view.

753
01:36:03.080 --> 01:36:05.140
Raúl Kripalani: which is then given to the B2B,

754
01:36:05.600 --> 01:36:10.530
Raúl Kripalani: And it, it is managed by the B2BP stack.

755
01:36:11.340 --> 01:36:17.590
Raúl Kripalani: So, just kind of, like, recapping, that's… The… the capability discovery?

756
01:36:18.010 --> 01:36:20.689
Raúl Kripalani: The other thing is the stream routing.

757
01:36:21.280 --> 01:36:26.700
Raúl Kripalani: So, already kind of, like, went through this, but… This is basically…

758
01:36:26.980 --> 01:36:40.580
Raúl Kripalani: What it looks like, so we look at the direction, if it's unidirectional, then we know it's EP2P. If it's bi-directional, then we evaluate, is it lip B2P or EP2P? If it contains the… if it starts with a slash after the U variant.

759
01:36:41.510 --> 01:36:48.880
Raúl Kripalani: And, yeah, this is how it all looks together. It is very simple in terms of code.

760
01:36:49.960 --> 01:36:54.780
Raúl Kripalani: As I said, the slash is reserved, it's a reserved character, and then the… the…

761
01:36:55.220 --> 01:37:10.539
Raúl Kripalani: So this is kind of, like, when it… this is… describes… what I've described so far is the connection management, so the connection creation, right? Listening a connection, and listening for a stream, right? So these are the list… what I've described are kind of, like, the listening side, the inbound side.

762
01:37:12.350 --> 01:37:19.600
Raúl Kripalani: And I wanted to go through some other things that we need to make… that we need to do, to make sure that the whole solution is sound.

763
01:37:19.930 --> 01:37:26.659
Raúl Kripalani: So… on one end, The listening, the management of the life cycle of the listener.

764
01:37:27.340 --> 01:37:29.140
Raúl Kripalani: the Transport Listen.

765
01:37:29.420 --> 01:37:44.839
Raúl Kripalani: is gonna be called by both sides, so Libby2P will get a quick transport, and then it will call listen on that transport, it will get a quick listener, and then it will start listening for streams. I don't know if rust does something similar, and what the rust

766
01:37:44.880 --> 01:37:51.119
Raúl Kripalani: do, but this is kind of, like, the thing that I'm familiar with, and it can be used kind of, like, as a point of reference.

767
01:37:51.640 --> 01:37:58.819
Raúl Kripalani: So, the idea here is… As soon as you get the first listen from one end.

768
01:37:59.370 --> 01:38:11.260
Raúl Kripalani: The shared transport is going to return an immediate listener, but it only begins actually listening on the underlying quick connection when both of them have attached, so that none… no side actually misses.

769
01:38:11.390 --> 01:38:17.429
Raúl Kripalani: any… any… any connections, any internet connections that might be for them. That's the way we remove

770
01:38:17.680 --> 01:38:19.699
Raúl Kripalani: The… the race, right?

771
01:38:20.250 --> 01:38:25.589
Raúl Kripalani: On the other side, we need to think about dials. So, both stacks are gonna dial.

772
01:38:25.940 --> 01:38:27.910
Raúl Kripalani: If Libby2P is dialing.

773
01:38:28.340 --> 01:38:38.589
Raúl Kripalani: then we're gonna do a handshake, we're gonna learn if the other party supports ETH B2B. If it does, we deliver the connection to both stacks.

774
01:38:40.320 --> 01:38:57.139
Raúl Kripalani: If ETHP2P is dialing, this is the interesting case, because ETHP2P wants to establish a connection with a peer, and the peer… say the peer supports ETH P2P, we just split the connection, and we get the… we do the same thing, as here, we give it to the P2P, and also to ETHP2P.

775
01:38:57.550 --> 01:39:01.659
Raúl Kripalani: But if the peer does not support EF B2BP, we have an opportunity.

776
01:39:02.090 --> 01:39:11.789
Raúl Kripalani: we can still deliver the raw B2P connection, given that we've gone through the handshake, and we've established a connection, we can offer it to the B2P side.

777
01:39:11.940 --> 01:39:20.499
Raúl Kripalani: But, so far, I've implemented it as only best effort, so if the Libby2P side is backlogged, it will…

778
01:39:20.900 --> 01:39:23.940
Raúl Kripalani: we simply drop it, and that's it. Nothing happened.

779
01:39:24.170 --> 01:39:25.050
Raúl Kripalani: Right?

780
01:39:26.370 --> 01:39:32.200
Raúl Kripalani: What do we do on connection close? Both of these tags can… might want to close their… their connection.

781
01:39:32.590 --> 01:39:36.019
Raúl Kripalani: So, closing only one side leaves the other one alive.

782
01:39:37.640 --> 01:39:41.210
Raúl Kripalani: And this is not implemented yet, but it's an idea.

783
01:39:41.550 --> 01:39:53.910
Raúl Kripalani: we could send an advisory to EP2P. We cannot do this with EIP2P, because, as we said, we want to… we don't wanna… don't wanna break APIs or impose new… new, requirements to clients. We want it to be completely transparent.

784
01:39:54.370 --> 01:39:59.870
Raúl Kripalani: So, but EP2P can get an advisory of, hey, this connection,

785
01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:06.080
Raúl Kripalani: It might, like, might be deprioritized or something like that, and then it can decide what it does.

786
01:40:06.210 --> 01:40:14.769
Raúl Kripalani: well, it can ask the application what it does, because that's the point of ETH B2BP as a design principle. It's not autonomous. It is always delegating to the application.

787
01:40:17.050 --> 01:40:34.219
Raúl Kripalani: And, yeah, so the connection… otherwise, the connection closes when both views are released, on the connection. And then the last gauge that we need to think about is what happens when listener… listener close. So, you got a listener from Transport Listen, and now you close it.

788
01:40:34.600 --> 01:40:36.940
Raúl Kripalani: So, what we've…

789
01:40:37.040 --> 01:40:43.210
Raúl Kripalani: what I decided here, after kind of, like, playing with different designs, is that I'm going to take the simplest path.

790
01:40:43.500 --> 01:40:48.680
Raúl Kripalani: And that is, I'm gonna make… a listen from GoP2P.

791
01:40:49.280 --> 01:40:50.890
Raúl Kripalani: Sorry, from EIPIP.

792
01:40:51.200 --> 01:40:56.390
Raúl Kripalani: illegal, a close… illegal, and also from EFP to B.

793
01:40:57.150 --> 01:41:02.079
Raúl Kripalani: Why? Because… A… closing a listener.

794
01:41:02.390 --> 01:41:09.100
Raúl Kripalani: is not done dynamically. It is done… it is part of the client life cycle.

795
01:41:09.270 --> 01:41:21.309
Raúl Kripalani: Right? So, when a client is staring down, is shutting down, it's going to tear down all its services, and one of them is going to be networking, probably the first one that it turns down, that it, that it tears down.

796
01:41:21.530 --> 01:41:25.269
Raúl Kripalani: So, the client actually controls the sequence, the shutdown sequence.

797
01:41:25.760 --> 01:41:34.360
Raúl Kripalani: So, what the client can do is just simply close the shared transport, And only then.

798
01:41:34.640 --> 01:41:37.810
Raúl Kripalani: send a close to the B2B and to EF B2B.

799
01:41:38.430 --> 01:41:42.599
Raúl Kripalani: Who will then go and close their listeners, and it's all clean.

800
01:41:43.030 --> 01:41:58.269
Raúl Kripalani: The other alternative added, in my opinion, a lot of complexity, because… and we have to think about, like, if you allow this, then the listener could be half closed, we need to detach the queue from the listener, and then…

801
01:41:58.510 --> 01:42:16.439
Raúl Kripalani: Like, you need to deal with, I've announced this capability, two other peers, peers are expecting something of me, but I'm really not listening. It just doesn't make sense, because it's never going to happen in reality at runtime, that you only want to close one part without closing the other. Or, also, what cannot happen, and what is not going to happen in reality.

802
01:42:16.440 --> 01:42:20.319
Raúl Kripalani: is a re-listen. There's no… as far as I know, there is no…

803
01:42:20.880 --> 01:42:30.329
Raúl Kripalani: happening inside a ETH client, or any, like, circumstance, where they would want to close a listener, and then reopen it later.

804
01:42:30.450 --> 01:42:36.819
Raúl Kripalani: it just simply… simply doesn't… as far as I know, I'm not… I'm not aware, so… so let me know if, like, you know, if… if I…

805
01:42:36.930 --> 01:42:42.440
Raúl Kripalani: If I've got wrong information here, because that would be… that would be valuable.

806
01:42:42.930 --> 01:42:52.350
Raúl Kripalani: And finally, ideally, eventually, the transport, the connection, and the handshakes should… should also be shared with the EL. The EL has this,

807
01:42:52.680 --> 01:43:08.279
Raúl Kripalani: in the Deputy REPL, there is this effort to also standardize QUIC as a… or to introduce support for QUIC. The handshake works a little different there, because the EL wants to support, web transport connections, so, in some

808
01:43:08.500 --> 01:43:10.180
Raúl Kripalani: And, and the…

809
01:43:10.290 --> 01:43:23.119
Raúl Kripalani: and the… and the extension, so the X509 extension that Libby2B uses to certify the peer's identity, to send a peer ID certificate, or to send a peer pump key certificate.

810
01:43:23.300 --> 01:43:26.269
Raúl Kripalani: Is not something that a…

811
01:43:26.380 --> 01:43:30.750
Raúl Kripalani: browser that a website could read, so it kind of, like.

812
01:43:30.920 --> 01:43:45.439
